Elements

.standard language for creating and structuring web pages using tags and elements
.defines how content like text, images, and links appear in a browser
.markup language - it annotates text to define how it is structured and displayed in web browsers
.static - doesn't provide interactive features 
    .can be combined with CSS for styling and JS for interactivity


.complete structure with tags and content
    <p class='text'>Hello Adagga</p>
.tag - the actual keyword/name enclosed in angle brackets that tells the browser what kind of context to expect 

<html> - root element; contains all the other tags 
<head> - section containing info like the page title 
<body> - section containing the visible content shown in the browser 

Elements
.are the basic building blocks of a webpage 
.define structure and content using tags 
.can contain text, attributes, or other nested elements
.some are self-closing 
.are read by the browser to determine how the page is rendered 
.not case sensitive 
.Nested elements
    .those placed inside other elements creating clear hierarchical structure for proper content organization and display
.Empty elements 
    .those without any content; don't print anything 
    .don't have an ending tag 
.Block-level elements
    .those that start on a new line
    .occupy the full available width 
    .stack vertically
    .can contain inline elements 

.Inline elements 
    .do not start on a new line/ stay within the text flow 
    .take the width of their content 
    .used within block-level elements to add or style content

